'' A POINTER PRINTS AS AN UNSIGNED INTEGER, AND THE CONVERSION IS AN INSTRUCTION.
''
'' PrintRtlFor already chose fb_PrintULongint for a pointer -- measured, that is what
'' fbc calls -- but PrintOne coerced the value to 'LowerType( t )', which for a pointer
'' is MT_PTR: a no-op. The ptr went into the i64 parameter unchanged and clang refused
'' the module outright with "'%t8' defined with type 'ptr' but expected 'i64'". Not a
'' wrong number -- no object at all, for any program that prints a pointer.
''
'' Measured with fbc (fbc64 -r), which writes the cast into the call itself:
''
''     print o.b     fb_PrintULongint( 0, *(uint64*)&O$0, 1 )
''     print ap      fb_PrintULongint( 0, (uint64)AP$0, 1 )
''     print fp      fb_PrintULongint( 0, (uint64)FP$0, 1 )
''
'' A PROCEDURE POINTER TAKES THE SAME PATH as a data one, which is why one is here:
'' TK_PROC shares the branch, and nothing else in the suite prints one.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/codegen/src/print-pointer.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' THE DECLARATION IS THE CONTRACT the call has to match. It is written from the
'' entry point's own signature, so it cannot drift with the argument.
'' EXPECT-OUT: declare void @"fb_PrintULongint"(i32, i64, i32)

'' AND THE CONVERSION ITSELF. 'ptrtoint' is the instruction Coerce emits for MT_PTR to
'' the pointer-sized integer; asserting only the call would pass again the moment the
'' operand went back to being a raw ptr, because the call TEXT is the same either way.
'' EXPECT-OUT: = ptrtoint ptr

'' NO CALL TAKES A 'ptr' IN THAT SLOT. This is the assertion that actually failed
'' before the fix -- the four prints emitted four of these.
'' EXPECT-NOT: @"fb_PrintULongint"(i32 0, ptr
'' GOLDEN: expected/print-pointer.out
